# rule.forbid.purposeless-passages

> **no passage exists because a form asked for it. a passage exists because a reader needs it.**

the clamp on `rule.require.purpose-first` — that rule names the target, this names the smell.

## .the lead smell — the header a template handed you

a template that lists a dozen questions produces a dozen headers, and each header pulls prose to fill it. the author never chose to pad; the form paid for length.

⇒ measured — *the outcome world* · *the day in the life* · *the before/after* · *the aha*: four sections, one fact, and a 4.5k wish grown to 155.8k of yields.

🟡 **so it is the only rule in the canon that can shrink an artifact before a word is typed** — the only one that grades a container the author was handed rather than prose the author wrote.

⇒ the repair is not a trim. write `none` and drop the section — a section padded to look thorough is noise with a header on it.

## .the shapes

| shape | 👎 what it is | 👍 the repair |
|---|---|---|
| the template header | a section that exists because the prompt named it | write `none`. drop it |
| the lap | reaches its purpose the long way | cut to the line that carries it |
| the displaced passage | true, tight, well-made — and aimed at a different purpose | **move it** to the passage whose purpose it serves |
| **the restated `.what`** | a header, then a sentence that says the header again | drop one |
| the courtesy section | *"## .risks — none identified"*, kept for symmetry | drop it |
| the parallel fill | three peers got a section, so the fourth got one too | the fourth may have naught to say |

🟡 **the displaced passage survives every other rule** — true, tight, bulletized, so no rule that grades a property of the words fires on it.

⇒ only a stated purpose reveals it is aimed elsewhere, and its repair is a move where `require.reflexive-condensation`'s is always a cut.

## .the test

> **name this passage's purpose as the READER'S decision. does the passage serve it?**

directly → keep · the long way → cut to the line that carries it · **it serves a different purpose well → move it there** · you cannot name one → cut it.

🟡 *"to explain the reviewer"* is a subject and grades naught. *"so an adopter can decide whether to wire it"* is a purpose, and it grades every line.

| when… | then… |
|---|---|
| a **template** or stage prompt named this section | the strongest cue. state its purpose, or write `none` |
| you would write *"for completeness"* or *"worth a mention"* | the passage just told you it has no purpose |
| a section is short and you feel it looks thin | thin is correct where the answer is thin. do not pad a header |
| a passage is true, tight, and you cannot cut it | check whether it is **displaced**. move, do not delete |
| you would call a passage *"too long"* | long compared to *what?* name the purpose first |
| a peer section has three bullets and yours has one | one is the right count where one is the truth |

## 🟡 .the boundary

| a violation | not a violation |
|---|---|
| a section written because the prompt listed it | a section written because a reader must make a decision |
| a passage whose purpose you cannot name | a passage whose purpose demands more words than you like |
| a header, then a restatement of the header | a header, then the claim it introduces |

**the line that parts them: would a reader ACT differently once they read it?** no → purposeless, however true. yes → it earns its header.


blocker: a section that exists because a template named it · a passage whose purpose the author cannot state · a displaced passage left where its purpose is not served · a header followed by a restatement of itself.
nitpick: a passage that laps its purpose where one line would carry it.
false positive: a short section whose answer is genuinely short · a passage whose purpose demands its length.

⇒ see also: `rule.require.purpose-first` · `define.purpose-first` · `rule.require.place-a-passage-by-its-subject` · `rule.require.reflexive-condensation`.
